# @PACKAGE_NAME@

[中文说明](README.md)

This is your design directory. In normal use, you only edit the RTL and the
two test files. You do not need to change `FrameTop.sv` or the root RTL files.

## Files in this directory

| File | Purpose | Edit it? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `rtl/@MODULE@.sv` | Your circuit | Yes; replace the example logic |
| `tests/@MODULE@Tb.sv` | Tests only your circuit | Yes; check your real behavior |
| `tests/Frame@MODULE@Tb.sv` | Tests your circuit through the full frame | Yes; check pins and design selection |
| `design.json` | Tells the build tools where sources and tests are | Usually no |

The design ID is `@DESIGN_ID@`, and the top module is `@MODULE@`.

## Step 1: Implement the circuit

Open `rtl/@MODULE@.sv` and replace the example logic. Keep the five top-level
ports. They are the fixed connection between your circuit and the frame:

- `clock`: clock input. A combinational circuit may ignore it, but keep it.
- `reset`: reset input. A circuit without reset may ignore it, but keep it.
- `io_in[65:0]`: 66 input values read from the external pins.
- `io_out[65:0]`: 66 values your circuit wants to send to external pins.
- `io_oe[65:0]`: output switches. Bit `n` must be 1 to drive pin `n`.

`io_*[n]` maps to `user_io[n + 7]` at the chip top. For example, `io_in[0]`
maps to `user_io[7]`.

## Step 2: Update the tests

Both TB files mark the sections that are normally kept and the sections that
you should edit for your design.

First update `tests/@MODULE@Tb.sv`, then run:

```sh
make design-lint DESIGN=designs/@DESIGN_ID@
make design-test DESIGN=designs/@DESIGN_ID@ TEST=io
```

The test passes only when its `$display` prints `PASS` and make exits normally.

## Step 3: Connect the design to FrameTop

After the standalone test passes, add `"@DESIGN_ID@/design.json"` to the root
`designs/registry.json`, then run:

```sh
make registry-generate
make registry-check
make design-frame-test DESIGN=designs/@DESIGN_ID@ TEST=frame
```

Before submitting, run:

```sh
make regression-fast
```

Verilator 5.050 is recommended, and 5.032 is also tested. The build scripts
skip nonessential warning flags that an older version does not recognize.
Versions older than 5.032 are not tested.

See `docs/user-guide.en.md` for the complete English workflow.
